sevillad@gmail.com wrote:

> I've been playing for a few days and I've finally come to a situation
> where I've accumulated a lot of junk. The problem is not the weight,
> but the quantity; I have repeated potions, darts, and other stuff,
> with separate letters, so I can't pick up anything else. Is there a
> way to stack similar objects? (#adjust swaps letters instead, I
> haven't seen any other command).

#adjust will stack objects that are *the same*; if they don't stack,
they're not. Also, if some objects are identified and some aren't,
they'll go in two separate stacks even if they *are* the same.

Your problem is, for instance: 

a - 5 uncursed darts
b - 5 darts
c - 2 darts
d - a blessed dart
e - 2 cursed -2 darts
f - 7 +0 darts

(b) and (c) are completely unknown. They may be uncursed, cursed or
blessed; they may have any enchantment.

(a) and (d) have known buc (blessed/uncursed/cursed) status. They have
been dropped on an altar so you know that, but not the enchantment.

(e) and (f) are fully identified: once the enchantment of something is
known, it doesn't need "uncursed" specified any more.

When you establish the buc status of (b) and (c), and they turn out to
have the same enchantment as something else with known buc status in your
inventory *that you don't know yet*, they will stack with that. You still
need to #adjust (to its own letter is okay) to make it stack; that's not
automatic.

When you identify something previously unidentified, you can #adjust it to
something with the same buc status and the same enchantment as something
else in your inventory. 

Suppose you identify (a) and (b) as uncursed +0 darts; then you can
#adjust all your uncursed +0 darts to the same letter.

> Also, sometimes things are stacked automatically (I have one pile with
> 3 darts and another one with 11). 

That happens when they have the same buc status, the same enchantment, and
the same level of identification.

